 \par
\par
This scene constitutes a kind of dramatic climax in the play\rquote s action: finally, Prospero\rquote s ambitions seem to be realised and he appears to be in complete control of the world around him. Raising the tempest at the start of the play seems to have worked out beautifully and, as he says later, \ldblquote At this hour/Lie at my mercy all mine enemies\'85\rdblquote . It is with a shock, then, that we realise that we are only in Act 3: there are just over two Acts of the play still to go and it is this lack of dramatic jeopardy that gives this play its distinctive tone. (The action of the play is, after all, \ldblquote stage managed\rdblquote  by Prospero throughout \endash  an idea that reaches its culmination in Peter Greenaway\rquote s Prospero\rquote s Books, where the lines of the characters are spoken aloud by Prospero, too.) But the sense of enormous power that is communicated in this scene \endash  the thunder, lightning and Ariel\rquote s harpy impersonation - come together with terrifying effect at this point in the play (the dramatic power that can be experienced in the theatre in this scene was communicated particularly effectively in Greg Doran\rquote s 2016 RSC production, which employed the latest live-motion-capture technology to immerse the audience in the experience that so terrifies the Italian noblemen) \endash  in a wholly successful production of The Tempest, I think that the audience should be as entranced and awe-struck by Prospero\rquote s magic as the characters on stage at this juncture. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Yet any stage effect is limited in its duration and one of the most notable aspects of this scene is how unmoved Antonio and Sebastian are by the extraordinary scene that they have just witnessed. Having initially taken arms against the harpy in an act of profound futility, they now have to place their weapons aside as their \par
\par
 \par
\par
\'85swords are now too massy for [their] strengths                                                         And will not be uplifted\'85    \par
\par
(The Tempest, III.iii.63-64) \par
\par
 \par
\par
 \par
\par
but at the end of this extract, the pair seem ready once again to offer resistance to the spirits that Prospero has summoned: \par
\par
 \par
\par
SEBASTIAN But one fiend at a time, I\rquote ll fight their legions o\rquote er. \par
\par
ANTONIO I\rquote ll be thy second.     \par
\par
(The Tempest, III.iii.23-24) \par
\par
 \par
\par
This resistance to the vision inspired by Prospero\rquote s magic sets Antonio and Sebastian apart from Alonso, the King of Naples, for whom the appearance of Ariel seems to have been completely devastating. One cannot help seeing the pair\rquote s resolute determination not to be moved by the harpy\rquote s appearance as mapping directly onto Antonio and Sebastian\rquote s stubborn refusal to respond to Prospero\rquote s clemency at the end of the play. The deflationary, cynical rhetoric of Antonio and Sebastian in the play reveals their ethical emptiness. Even when faced by an astonishing act of magnanimity and kindness at the end of the play - \par
\par
 \par
\par
PROSPERO For you, most wicked sir, whom to call brother \par
Would even infect my mouth, I do forgive \par
Thy rankest fault; all of them\'85   \par
\par
(The Tempest, V.i.130-132) \par
\par
 \par
\par
- Antonio and Sebastian stand mute. Many of the play\rquote s critics have seen this as allying with the way in which there are always going to be \ldblquote men of sin\rdblquote  (III.iii.7) who are tone deaf when hearing the Christian message which has Christ\rquote s infinite mercy at its heart. While Alonso\rquote s ability to be moved by the spectacle presented in this scene will be rewarded with the restoration of his Kingdom and, miraculously, the return of his son from what must have seemed certain death, Antonio and Sebastian will end the play as they began it: their final lines in the play  \par
\par
 \par
\par
SEBASTIAN                       \'85Ha, ha! \par
What things are these, my lord Antonio? \par
Will money buy 'em? \par
\par
ANTONIO\f0\u8239?                 Very like; one of them \par
Is a plain fish, and, no doubt, marketable. \par
\par
(The Tempest, V.i.130-132) \par
\par
 \par
\par
are saturated in the cynicism and the world weariness that has characterized their utterances throughout the text. It would seem that these two characters\rquote  refusal to experience change is the real \ldblquote\'85Ling\rquote ring perdition, worse than any death\'85\rdblquote  (III.iii.33) referred to by Ariel in his speech in this passage. \par
