9 \lquote Powerful emotions are often the excuse for unacceptable behaviour.\rquote   \par
\par
In the light of this view, consider ways in which writers explore the connection between intense thoughts and bad deeds.  \par
\par
In your answer, compare one drama text and one poetry text from the above lists. [30] \par
\par
 \par
\par
Ferdinand\rquote s outburst on hearing of his sister\rquote s marriage is an obvious place to begin this consideration of \ldblquote powerful emotion[ ]\rdblquote  and its influence on terrible moral behaviour in Webster\rquote s Jacobean Tragedy The Duchess of Malfi (1614) and John Milton\rquote s Christian Epic Paradise Lost (1667). When Ferdinand, The Duke of Calabria, hears that his sister the eponymous Duchess of Malfi has married in secret and has subsequently had children his reaction is terrifying:  \par
\par
 \par
\par
FERDINAND: \'85I would have their bodies [i.e. the bodies of the Duchess\rquote s children] \par
Burnt in a coal-pit with the\f0\u8239?ventage\u8239?stopped\'85 \par
Or dip the sheets they lie in in pitch or sulphur, \par
Wrap them in\rquote t, and then light them like a match\'85 \par
\par
(The Duchess of Malfi, II.v.66-70) \par
\par
 \par
\par
\ldblquote Unacceptable\rdblquote  seems a mild word to describe Ferdinand\rquote s totally unhinged behaviour at this point in the play. The diabolical note that one hears in this scene links Ferdinand with Milton\rquote s Satan whose default mode often seems to be the self-pitying, intemperate tirade. Here is Satan responding to the beauties of Eden in Book 9: \par
\par
 \par
\par
With what delight could I have\u8239?walked\u8239?thee round, \par
If I could joy in aught, sweet interchange\u8239? \par
Of hill, and\u8239?valley, rivers, woods and\u8239?plains\'85 \par
                          \'85but I in none of these \par
Find place or refuge; and the more I see \par
Pleasures about me, so much more I feel\u8239? \par
Torment within me, as from the hateful siege \par
Of contraries; all good to me becomes \par
Bane\'85 \par
\par
(Paradise Lost, IX.114-123)  \par
\par
 \par
\par
These two speeches seem linked by the speakers\rquote  lack of self-knowledge: Ferdinand seems unaware that his world-transforming hatred for his sister and her family is prompted by his own unacknowledged incestuous feeling for his twin sibling while Satan seems totally unaware that his own terrible predicament is the direct result of his own steadfast refusal to obey God\rquote s will (\ldblquote\'85Better to reign in hell, than serve in heaven\'85\rdblquote  I.263). C.S. Lewis has pointed out the foolishness in Satan\rquote s refusal to accept his subordinate role to God, drawing our attention to the fact that \ldblquote the Satanic predicament consists in\'85Satan viewing himself as suffering from a \lquote sense of injured merit\rquote  (I.98)\'85\rdblquote  This resentment is, Lewis points out, totally baseless and without foundation, emerging naturally out of Satan\rquote s temperament \f1\endash  Satan is incapable of \ldblquote intense thought[ ]\rdblquote  but is, rather, a victim of his own \ldblquote powerful feeling[ ]\rdblquote  of having his intrinsic merit overlooked by a tyrannical God. Lewis views Blake and Shelley as credulous dupes who have taken Satan\rquote s pathetic self-pity (\ldblquote\'85With what delight could I have\f0\u8239?walked\u8239?thee round\'85\rdblquote ) at face value. If one accepts such a reading of Satan\rquote s character, the connection with Ferdinand, the Duke of Calabria, is an obvious one: it is, after all,  hardly reasonable to assume that the Duchess would reciprocate her brother\rquote s sexual feelings for her (such a scenario would have to wait for John Ford\rquote s \lquote Tis Pity She\rquote s a Whore in 1630) so his outrage at her remarriage is simply a function of his crazed narcissism. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Satan\rquote s behaviour being prompted by an irrational \ldblquote sense of injured merit\rdblquote  (I.98) links the character neatly with that other Malcontent, Bosola. From the moment he sets foot onstage, the latter is the source of a whole host of complaints \f1\endash  the world has, it seems, failed to recognize Bosola\rquote s peculiar gifts: \par
\par
 \par
\par
Bosola. \tab\tab\'85I have done you \par
Better service than to be slighted thus. \par
(The Duchess of Malfi, I.i.29-30) \par
\par
 \par
\par
In a sense, Bosola\rquote s resentment \endash  his sense of injured pride \endash  can be seen as the motive force of the play (just as Satan\rquote s sense of injured pride can be seen as initiating the action that prompts the plot of Paradise Lost). In both cases, I think, the reader (or viewer) recognizes that the characters\rquote  righteous fury is misplaced, disproportionate and absurd. Satan and Bosola\rquote s dedication to \ldblquote unacceptable behaviour\rdblquote  seems complete and unconditional \endash  the former cannot bear even to behold goodness (\ldblquote\'85all good to me becomes/Bane\'85\rdblquote ) while the latter is responsible for the most shocking moment in The Duchess of Malfi, announcing casually \ldblquote Some other strangle the children\rdblquote  in Act 4, Scene 2.      \par
\par
  \par
\par
It can be argued that the most catastrophic moment in human history, The Fall, takes place because of both an overwhelming outburst of strong emotion and through a failure of the human intellect, wrongheaded thinking. If Adam eats the apple because of powerful feeling for his partner (feelings that would be identified by Milton\rquote s readers as an example of the medieval sin of uxoriousness), Eve falls because she is intellectually seduced by Satan, believing his manifest lies because they flatter her vanity. Satan is keen to present Eve herself as an underappreciated deity, depicting Eden as a backwater unworthy to contain such a \ldblquote celestial\f0\u8239?beauty\rdblquote  (IX.540): \par
\par
 \par
\par
Who sees thee? (and what is one?) who\u8239?shouldst\u8239?be seen \par
A Goddess among Gods,\u8239?adored\u8239?and\u8239?served \par
By angels numberless\'85 \par
\par
(Paradise Lost, IX.546-548)  \par
\par
 \par
\par
So Satan successfully replicates in Eve the \ldblquote sense of injured merit\rdblquote  that was identified by Lewis as the defining feature of his own character. The equivalent scene in The Duchess of Malfi sees the eponymous character resist the empty blandishments of Bosola, who tries to convince her that life is essentially an empty, nihilistic experience: \par
\par
 \par
\par
Bosola.  \'85Thou art a box of\u8239?worm-seed - at best, but a\u8239?salvatory of green mummy. What\rquote s this flesh? A little\u8239?crudded\u8239?milk fantastical\u8239?puff-paste...\u8239? \par
\par
(The Duchess of Malfi, IV.ii.120-121) \par
\par
 \par
\par
At this point in the play we can see that \ldblquote powerful emotion\rdblquote  can lead to actions that would be regarded as profoundly moral by Webster\rquote s audience: the Duchess\rquote s resistance to Bosola\rquote s worldview is founded on her powerful religious belief: \par
\par
 \par
\par
BOSOLA: Doth not death fright you? \par
\par
DUCHESS: Who would be afraid on\rquote t, \par
Knowing to meet such excellent company \par
In th\rquote  other world? \par
\par
(The Duchess of Malfi, IV.ii.120-121) \par
\par
 \par
\par
The corresponding scene in Paradise Lost sees the first human couple fall to their knees, abject before God\rquote s power. As Adam and Eve cry for mercy, we recognize that Milton\rquote s purpose in his Christian Epic is to \ldblquote justify\u8239?the ways of God to men\rdblquote  (I.26).  \par
\par
 \par
\par
\'85with\u8239?their\u8239?sighs the air \par
Frequenting, sent from hearts contrite, in sign \par
Of sorrow\u8239?unfeigned, and humiliation meek.  \par
\par
(Paradise Lost, X.1102-1104) \par
\par
 \par
\par
Both writers, Webster and Milton, however different their original purposes, and however distinct the effects achieved within their works, save their most \ldblquote powerful emotions\rdblquote  for the affirmation of the Christian message \f1\endash  those who commit \ldblquote bad deeds\rdblquote  are wholly destroyed and those who live according to the tenets of Christianity are, ultimately, rewarded.  \par
